A||||8||rogergarrison||Wheel Adapters||||1||
Z||000000||rogergarrison||09-17-2001||11:52 PM||||Just got an email from someone using wheel adapters to allow use of chevy wheels on his Fiero. All five studs seperated and his rear wheel passed him on the highway. Fortunatly, I dont think it caused an accident. Any of you out there using them should reconsider getting custom wheels made for Fiero bolt pattern. This is at least the 5th time I personnaly heard of it happening, once to me.||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||09-18-2001||07:26 AM||||Agreeed, if you won't do it for yourself, take them off your car so you don't kill ME  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Note: it is possible to have Fiero hubs modified to carry different bolt patterns. Quite reliably and withought killing anyone.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>Just got an email from someone using wheel adapters to allow use of chevy wheels on his Fiero. All five studs seperated and his rear wheel passed him on the highway. Fortunatly, I dont think it caused an accident. Any of you out there using them should reconsider getting custom wheels made for Fiero bolt pattern. This is at least the 5th time I personnaly heard of it happening, once to me.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||AusFiero||09-18-2001||08:04 AM||||Yes adaptors are a cheap and nasty alternative to just fitting a new bolt pattern. Why not redrill the wheels or do as artherd said. Have seen them give way also. Glad I wasn't in the way.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000003||3fifty||09-18-2001||08:59 PM||||Roger,<BR>It was me that sent you the mail. Not Fiero to Chevy. Fiero to Fiero just 2" wider. I had been doing about 70 earlier, but was down to about 40. Drug the rotor and backing plate. Just a small crack under the lip of the fender  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Please tell me where I can get<BR>7 and 10 inch wide custom wheels with the factory bolt pattern. I will be glad to send a pic to anyone who would like to see what an adapter will do to you. What if I had the<BR>350 in already? <P>3fify one day!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||09-18-2001||10:22 PM||||I'd like to know how the adapters were made, what they were made of, and who made them. I'd also like to know exactly how it broke. Did the studs pull through? Can you post a pic of the broken one? I guess I was lucky with my Countach, I never had a problem even with the weight of a 350/turbo325 and 10" wheels. But I didn't put more than 10k miles on it. I don't think they're too bad for a show car, but I wouldn't want them on my daily driver. Mine were from Fred Goeske at <A HREF="http://www.wheeladapter.com/manufacturing-wheel_adapters.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.wheeladapter.com/manufacturing-wheel_adapters.asp</A>  I was going to get them for my Testarossa too, but I'm leaning toward longer control arms and larger hubs. <P> You won't find 10" wide wheels with the 5x100 pattern unless you shell out ~$2000 for a set of Compomotives or similar. And not all wheels can be redrilled.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Countach||09-19-2001||12:42 AM||||Do you have pics of your Countach?  I'm building one myself... slowly!  If you're interested, I have pics at <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/countach711" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/countach711</A> <BR>||||reg||11
